# The negation of an interval with 2-limb bounds (milestone2.md, "Interval kernels", "Two limbs"):
# neg_2 of each bound, exchanged. Exact; the empty set ((+inf, 0), (-inf, 0)) is its own negation,
# with the signs of its zeros changed. A bounded operand gives a bounded result: a finite canonical
# 2-limb value is below the overflow threshold 2^(qmax + p) in magnitude (its leading limb is at
# most Omega and its second within half a gap of it, a tie going away from Omega), so every member
# of -X is, and the condition of bounded_when, with exp 0, holds exactly when the result is bounded.
# For every precision.
kernel:        interval_neg_2
input:         xl = xl0 xl1
input:         xu = xu0 xu1
output:        zl = zl0 zl1
output:        zu = zu0 zu1
requires:      interval xl xu
ensures:       encloses_interval zl zu -x x=xl,xu tight=exact bounded_when=abs_le(-x,0) bounded_exactly
min_precision: 1
source:        neg_2 of each bound, exchanged (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeIntervalExact.v interval_neg_2_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   sign changes only, from the upper bound to the lower one, and neg_2 is exempt; encloses_interval states what comes out for every canonical interval

(zl0, zl1) = neg_2(xu0, xu1)
(zu0, zu1) = neg_2(xl0, xl1)
